1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,875
A 50 lakh home, and a bill of 1 crore.

2
00:00:02,875 --> 00:00:04,153
This is not a mistake,

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:07,028
it's simple math of a 20-year home loan.

4
00:00:07,028 --> 00:00:09,584
The flat you buy for 50 lakh,

5
00:00:09,584 --> 00:00:12,139
you end up paying the bank almost double for it.

6
00:00:12,139 --> 00:00:14,056
And the strangest part is this,

7
00:00:14,056 --> 00:00:17,570
for the first several years, the house is not really yours.

8
00:00:17,570 --> 00:00:20,810
Listen to the story of Amit and Neha. Both of them have jobs,

9
00:00:20,810 --> 00:00:22,753
and they have a little daughter at home.

10
00:00:22,753 --> 00:00:26,641
In their rented house, the landlord raised the rent every year.

11
00:00:26,641 --> 00:00:29,881
One evening Neha said, that's it, we'll buy our own house now.

12
00:00:29,881 --> 00:00:33,444
Her father agreed too, saying rent just goes to waste,

13
00:00:33,444 --> 00:00:34,740
and at least the EMI is our own.

14
00:00:34,740 --> 00:00:37,497
At the bank, Bank Uncle opened the file,

15
00:00:37,497 --> 00:00:41,043
and explained it all very calmly. A loan of 50 lakh,

16
00:00:41,043 --> 00:00:44,194
for 20 years, at 8 percent interest.

17
00:00:44,194 --> 00:00:48,133
The monthly EMI will be 41,822 rupees. Amit looked at his salary,

18
00:00:48,133 --> 00:00:51,679
did the math in his head, and thought, yes, we can manage. They signed,

19
00:00:51,679 --> 00:00:54,830
and within a few weeks the keys were in their hands.

20
00:00:54,830 --> 00:00:58,058
On the day of the housewarming everyone was happy. Mummy distributed sweets,

21
00:00:58,058 --> 00:01:00,963
and the daughter was running around in her new room.

22
00:01:00,963 --> 00:01:04,836
Nobody asked how much would be paid in total over 20 years.

23
00:01:04,836 --> 00:01:07,095
Because the EMI always looks like a small number.

24
00:01:07,095 --> 00:01:10,000
The total is the number that takes away your sleep.

25
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:11,093
3 years later,

26
00:01:11,093 --> 00:01:14,371
Amit opened the loan statement carefully for the first time.

27
00:01:14,371 --> 00:01:18,014
He was shocked. He had paid so many months of EMI,

28
00:01:18,014 --> 00:01:21,657
but the debt had come down only a little. He wondered,

29
00:01:21,657 --> 00:01:25,300
did the bank make a mistake? It was not a mistake,

30
00:01:25,300 --> 00:01:27,850
this is just how a home loan is designed.

31
00:01:27,850 --> 00:01:29,529
So today, 3 questions. The first,

32
00:01:29,529 --> 00:01:32,550
what is actually inside an EMI? The second,

33
00:01:32,550 --> 00:01:36,914
in the first 5 years, how much of the house is truly yours?

34
00:01:36,914 --> 00:01:37,250
The third,

35
00:01:37,250 --> 00:01:40,943
how much do you save by paying just 1 extra EMI in a year?

36
00:01:40,943 --> 00:01:44,300
And finally, the right that the bank often doesn't tell you about.

37
00:01:44,300 --> 00:01:45,955
First question, what is an EMI?

38
00:01:45,955 --> 00:01:48,272
EMI means the fixed monthly installment

39
00:01:48,272 --> 00:01:50,259
that you pay to the bank.

40
00:01:50,259 --> 00:01:53,569
Inside every EMI there is principal, and there is interest too.

41
00:01:53,569 --> 00:01:56,549
Principal means the actual loan, the money you took from the bank.

42
00:01:56,549 --> 00:02:00,190
Interest means the rent on that loan, which the bank charges you.

43
00:02:00,190 --> 00:02:04,044
Now listen to the most important part. On what does the bank charge interest?

44
00:02:04,044 --> 00:02:06,497
On the loan that is still pending.

45
00:02:06,497 --> 00:02:08,949
At the start, the full 50 lakh is pending,

46
00:02:08,949 --> 00:02:11,402
so the interest is also on the full 50 lakh.

47
00:02:11,402 --> 00:02:15,957
That's why most of the first EMI goes straight into interest.

48
00:02:15,957 --> 00:02:18,760
Very little goes toward the home loan itself.

49
00:02:18,760 --> 00:02:21,670
Neha understood it like a household budget.

50
00:02:21,670 --> 00:02:23,933
Put a pile of 41,822 rupees on the table.

51
00:02:23,933 --> 00:02:27,167
The bank first takes its interest out of that.

52
00:02:27,167 --> 00:02:30,400
Whatever is left is the only part that reduces your loan.

53
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,987
In the early years, this leftover part

54
00:02:32,987 --> 00:02:35,250
is just a small corner of the pile.

55
00:02:35,250 --> 00:02:37,705
As the loan reduces a little,

56
00:02:37,705 --> 00:02:41,564
the interest also reduces a little. But the EMI stays the same.

57
00:02:41,564 --> 00:02:45,423
So every month the principal part keeps growing a little.

58
00:02:45,423 --> 00:02:48,580
Drop by drop at the start, a full stream in the last years.

59
00:02:48,580 --> 00:02:50,685
This schedule is called amortisation,

60
00:02:50,685 --> 00:02:53,140
meaning a map of how the loan slowly gets paid off.

61
00:02:53,140 --> 00:02:56,863
Understand it with colours. Red means interest, green means principal.

62
00:02:56,863 --> 00:03:00,959
In the early years, most of each EMI is red.

63
00:03:00,959 --> 00:03:03,193
In the last years, mostly green.

64
00:03:03,193 --> 00:03:06,171
It means the bank earns its money at the start.

65
00:03:06,171 --> 00:03:09,150
And the house becomes yours only at the end.

66
00:03:09,150 --> 00:03:11,894
Think of a loan at a grocery shop. Say the shopkeeper says,

67
00:03:11,894 --> 00:03:13,953
each month I'll first cut my interest,

68
00:03:13,953 --> 00:03:16,697
then reduce your account with the rest. You would say,

69
00:03:16,697 --> 00:03:18,413
this is backwards accounting.

70
00:03:18,413 --> 00:03:22,186
But in a home loan, exactly this happens. The difference is only this,

71
00:03:22,186 --> 00:03:25,960
here everything is already written on paper beforehand.

72
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:29,469
Now let's add up the total. 41,822 rupees, every month,

73
00:03:29,469 --> 00:03:31,223
for a full 20 years.

74
00:03:31,223 --> 00:03:35,170
Add it up and the bill comes to almost 1 crore.

75
00:03:35,170 --> 00:03:39,994
That is, 50 lakh for the house, and almost the same amount just in interest.

76
00:03:39,994 --> 00:03:44,819
This is that double we talked about in the beginning.

77
00:03:44,819 --> 00:03:47,450
And this is the bank's earning.

78
00:03:47,450 --> 00:03:49,905
Amit showed this calculation to Papa.

79
00:03:49,905 --> 00:03:52,359
Papa stayed quiet for a while, then said,

80
00:03:52,359 --> 00:03:55,866
this is why we were afraid of loans. Amit said, Papa,

81
00:03:55,866 --> 00:03:59,022
Don't be scared, just understand. A loan is not bad,

82
00:03:59,022 --> 00:04:02,177
a home is necessary. But letting it run for 20 years without understanding

83
00:04:02,177 --> 00:04:03,580
turns out to be expensive.

84
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:06,720
Second question. After the first 5 years of EMI,

85
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:10,558
how much of the house is yours? Most people think,

86
00:04:10,558 --> 00:04:14,396
I've paid EMI for 5 years, so a good chunk of the house is mine now.

87
00:04:14,396 --> 00:04:17,188
But if you look at the numbers, the truth is quite different.

88
00:04:17,188 --> 00:04:19,630
And this truth stings a little too.

89
00:04:19,630 --> 00:04:22,584
In the first 5 years, you pay a lot of EMI.

90
00:04:22,584 --> 00:04:25,243
But most of that money goes into interest.

91
00:04:25,243 --> 00:04:27,606
The actual loan only goes down a little.

92
00:04:27,606 --> 00:04:29,083
Picture the house in your mind,

93
00:04:29,083 --> 00:04:32,037
and even after 5 years, only one small corner is yours.

94
00:04:32,037 --> 00:04:34,400
The rest of the house is still in the bank's name.

95
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:37,931
Neha read every page of her loan statement carefully.

96
00:04:37,931 --> 00:04:41,815
In each month's line, interest was written separately and principal separately.

97
00:04:41,815 --> 00:04:45,700
In the early years, the interest part was bigger every time.

98
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:48,878
That day she understood that paying EMI and paying off the loan

99
00:04:48,878 --> 00:04:50,290
are not the same thing.

100
00:04:50,290 --> 00:04:53,677
This hurts the most when your plans change.

101
00:04:53,677 --> 00:04:56,756
Say after 5 years you get a job in another city.

102
00:04:56,756 --> 00:04:57,679
You have to sell the house.

103
00:04:57,679 --> 00:05:01,990
Then you find out the bank still has to get back almost the whole loan.

104
00:05:01,990 --> 00:05:04,145
A big part of all those years of EMI

105
00:05:04,145 --> 00:05:06,300
has already gone away as interest.

106
00:05:06,300 --> 00:05:09,633
Now one more thing that most people don't understand:

107
00:05:09,633 --> 00:05:12,632
floating rate. Floating rate means the interest is not fixed.

108
00:05:12,632 --> 00:05:14,632
It keeps going up and down

109
00:05:14,632 --> 00:05:18,298
with the RBI's repo rate. Repo rate means the interest

110
00:05:18,298 --> 00:05:21,630
at which the RBI lends money to banks. When that goes up,

111
00:05:21,630 --> 00:05:23,630
your interest goes up too.

112
00:05:23,630 --> 00:05:27,583
When interest goes up, the bank often doesn't raise your EMI.

113
00:05:27,583 --> 00:05:30,457
It quietly adds more years to the loan.

114
00:05:30,457 --> 00:05:34,051
You think everything is fine, the EMI is the same.

115
00:05:34,051 --> 00:05:36,925
But more months have been added behind the loan.

116
00:05:36,925 --> 00:05:39,800
And with every new month, more interest.

117
00:05:39,800 --> 00:05:42,284
One day Amit asked Bank Uncle,

118
00:05:42,284 --> 00:05:44,146
when will my loan finally end?

119
00:05:44,146 --> 00:05:47,251
He was shocked to hear the answer. After the interest went up,

120
00:05:47,251 --> 00:05:49,113
the final date had moved further ahead.

121
00:05:49,113 --> 00:05:51,597
Nobody told you this over a phone call.

122
00:05:51,597 --> 00:05:53,770
This is something you have to check yourself.

123
00:05:53,770 --> 00:05:55,319
So, straight talk for you.

124
00:05:55,319 --> 00:05:57,488
Every year, make sure you get your loan statement.

125
00:05:57,488 --> 00:05:59,346
See how much of the actual principal is still left.

126
00:05:59,346 --> 00:06:01,205
And see when the loan will finish.

127
00:06:01,205 --> 00:06:04,303
If this date keeps moving further ahead year after year,

128
00:06:04,303 --> 00:06:07,091
then understand that the interest meter is running fast.

129
00:06:07,091 --> 00:06:09,260
And now is the time to do something.

130
00:06:09,260 --> 00:06:11,488
Third question, and the most useful one.

131
00:06:11,488 --> 00:06:15,572
What happens if you pay just 1 extra EMI in a year?

132
00:06:15,572 --> 00:06:19,656
That is, on top of the year's EMIs, just 1 more EMI.

133
00:06:19,656 --> 00:06:22,255
It sounds like a small thing.

134
00:06:22,255 --> 00:06:26,710
But seeing its effect, both Amit and Neha were amazed.

135
00:06:26,710 --> 00:06:29,348
Neha used to get a little bonus every year.

136
00:06:29,348 --> 00:06:32,976
Earlier, that money would go on trips or new gadgets.

137
00:06:32,976 --> 00:06:36,604
This time she thought, why not put it into the loan?

138
00:06:36,604 --> 00:06:39,902
This is called prepayment. That is, on top of the fixed EMI,

139
00:06:39,902 --> 00:06:43,200
paying off some part of the loan early, by your own choice.

140
00:06:43,200 --> 00:06:45,021
Why does this work so well?

141
00:06:45,021 --> 00:06:47,146
Because the extra money doesn't go into interest.

142
00:06:47,146 --> 00:06:49,574
It directly reduces the principal.

143
00:06:49,574 --> 00:06:51,092
And when the principal goes down,

144
00:06:51,092 --> 00:06:53,824
the interest charged every month after that is also lower.

145
00:06:53,824 --> 00:06:56,252
This benefit doesn't come just once, it comes every month,

146
00:06:56,252 --> 00:06:57,770
and keeps going for years.

147
00:06:57,770 --> 00:07:01,609
Now listen to ET's calculation. A loan of 50 lakh,

148
00:07:01,609 --> 00:07:06,302
8 percent interest, 20 years. Just 1 extra EMI every year,

149
00:07:06,302 --> 00:07:07,582
that is, 41,822 rupees.

150
00:07:07,582 --> 00:07:11,848
This saves about 10.17 lakh rupees in interest.

151
00:07:11,848 --> 00:07:16,540
This is money that would otherwise have become the bank's earnings.

152
00:07:16,540 --> 00:07:19,382
And it's not just money, you save time too.

153
00:07:19,382 --> 00:07:23,644
According to ET, the loan finishes 41 months earlier.

154
00:07:23,644 --> 00:07:26,486
That is, 3 years 5 months fewer EMIs. Just think,

155
00:07:26,486 --> 00:07:30,393
more than 3 years with no tension about the monthly installment.

156
00:07:30,393 --> 00:07:33,590
That money can then go towards your own dreams.

157
00:07:33,590 --> 00:07:37,428
Neha asked herself a straight question. If the bonus goes on a gadget,

158
00:07:37,428 --> 00:07:41,266
the gadget is old in a few years. If the same bonus goes into the loan,

159
00:07:41,266 --> 00:07:44,406
lakhs in interest are saved, and the house becomes yours sooner.

160
00:07:44,406 --> 00:07:45,802
The decision wasn't hard.

161
00:07:45,802 --> 00:07:49,640
Simply put, nobody had put this calculation in front of them before.

162
00:07:49,640 --> 00:07:53,171
If taking out 41,822 rupees in one go feels hard, the method is simple.

163
00:07:53,171 --> 00:07:56,702
Every month, set aside a little money along with your EMI.

164
00:07:56,702 --> 00:08:00,233
By the end of the year, that becomes 1 extra EMI.

165
00:08:00,233 --> 00:08:04,085
Some banks also give an option to pay a little extra every month.

166
00:08:04,085 --> 00:08:05,690
Ask your bank.

167
00:08:05,690 --> 00:08:09,308
Now, one important thing. At the time of prepayment, the bank asks,

168
00:08:09,308 --> 00:08:11,841
reduce the EMI or reduce the years?

169
00:08:11,841 --> 00:08:14,735
Most people choose to reduce the EMI,

170
00:08:14,735 --> 00:08:17,268
because it looks like money is left in hand.

171
00:08:17,268 --> 00:08:19,800
But often more interest is saved

172
00:08:19,800 --> 00:08:23,780
when the years are reduced. Make this choice carefully.

173
00:08:23,780 --> 00:08:24,728
Now that thing,

174
00:08:24,728 --> 00:08:27,886
which is often not said loudly at the bank counter.

175
00:08:27,886 --> 00:08:31,361
Many people are afraid of prepayment because the bank will charge a penalty.

176
00:08:31,361 --> 00:08:33,888
Earlier, this did happen on many loans.

177
00:08:33,888 --> 00:08:36,099
But now RBI has a clear rule.

178
00:08:36,099 --> 00:08:38,310
And this rule is in your favour.

179
00:08:38,310 --> 00:08:40,407
According to the RBI rule,

180
00:08:40,407 --> 00:08:43,903
if you took the loan for yourself, not for business,

181
00:08:43,903 --> 00:08:46,000
and the loan is on a floating rate,

182
00:08:46,000 --> 00:08:49,146
then the bank cannot charge any prepayment fee.

183
00:08:49,146 --> 00:08:52,292
That means no penalty. Whenever you want, as much as you want,

184
00:08:52,292 --> 00:08:54,040
you can pay off your debt early.

185
00:08:54,040 --> 00:08:56,542
Which loans do these new rules apply to?

186
00:08:56,542 --> 00:08:59,356
Loans received on or after 1 January 2026.

187
00:08:59,356 --> 00:09:01,545
Or renewed after that day.

188
00:09:01,545 --> 00:09:04,672
So take out your loan agreement and check the date. If in doubt,

189
00:09:04,672 --> 00:09:07,799
ask the bank in writing. This is your right,

190
00:09:07,799 --> 00:09:09,050
not a favour from the bank.

191
00:09:09,050 --> 00:09:11,005
There is one more way, refinancing.

192
00:09:11,005 --> 00:09:14,525
That means shifting your loan to another bank

193
00:09:14,525 --> 00:09:18,044
that offers lower interest. In one ET calculation,

194
00:09:18,044 --> 00:09:21,563
the loan was 50 lakh for 20 years.

195
00:09:21,563 --> 00:09:24,692
Interest dropped from 8.5 to 7.5 percent.

196
00:09:24,692 --> 00:09:27,820
So about 7.47 lakh rupees were saved in interest.

197
00:09:27,820 --> 00:09:29,468
But before you shift,

198
00:09:29,468 --> 00:09:32,763
also add the new bank's processing fees and paperwork costs.

199
00:09:32,763 --> 00:09:35,070
Many times the old bank itself, when asked,

200
00:09:35,070 --> 00:09:38,365
reduces the interest. You just have to ask.

201
00:09:38,365 --> 00:09:41,990
A customer who stays silent often ends up stuck with the same interest rate.

202
00:09:41,990 --> 00:09:44,858
Now, the mistake most families make.

203
00:09:44,858 --> 00:09:48,363
They treat the EMI like rent. Pay every month,

204
00:09:48,363 --> 00:09:51,868
and forget about it. The statement is never opened, and no prepayment is ever made.

205
00:09:51,868 --> 00:09:55,055
They never talk to the bank about the interest.

206
00:09:55,055 --> 00:09:58,560
And 20 years later they find out the house cost them double.

207
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:01,290
So what should you do? Check your statement every year.

208
00:10:01,290 --> 00:10:03,240
When a bonus or any extra money comes in,

209
00:10:03,240 --> 00:10:05,970
put some part of it into prepayment.

210
00:10:05,970 --> 00:10:08,700
Use prepayment to reduce the years. If the interest goes up,

211
00:10:08,700 --> 00:10:12,990
talk to the bank. But keep your emergency money separate,

212
00:10:12,990 --> 00:10:16,890
don't throw everything into the loan. This is not investment advice.

213
00:10:16,890 --> 00:10:20,317
Amit and Neha have the same house, and the same EMI.

214
00:10:20,317 --> 00:10:24,087
It's just that now they're not walking in the dark. They know

215
00:10:24,087 --> 00:10:26,829
how much of each EMI goes where.

216
00:10:26,829 --> 00:10:29,570
And this is the difference between someone who takes a loan,

217
00:10:29,570 --> 00:10:33,340
and someone who understands a loan. Same house, but the bill is now smaller.

218
00:10:33,340 --> 00:10:39,347
In the next video we'll talk about the minimum due on credit cards,

219
00:10:39,347 --> 00:10:43,715
and the trap hidden inside it.

220
00:10:43,715 --> 00:10:47,538
But before that, I have one simple question for you.

221
00:10:47,538 --> 00:10:54,090
Have you ever opened and looked at your home loan statement?

222
00:10:54,090 --> 00:10:57,367
Yes or no, write in the comments.
